(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-06"] = {"6.1":{"s":"For any reaction we can ask three things: how far it goes (equilibrium), whether it can happen (thermodynamics) and how fast it goes. Chemical kinetics is the study of the third question: reaction rates and what controls them."},"6.2":{"s":"Rate of reaction is how fast reactants are used up or products formed: the change in concentration per unit time (mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹). Average rate is taken over an interval. Instantaneous rate is the slope of the concentration-time graph at one moment. To get one rate for the whole reaction, divide by the stoichiometric coefficients.","f":[{"x":"rate = -(1/a)*d[A]/dt = (1/c)*d[C]/dt","n":"Rate of aA + bB -> cC + dD: divide each rate of change by its coefficient; minus sign for reactants.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.2, p.121"}],"w":["The rate of consumption of B in A + 3B -> 2C is three times that of A; the rate of REACTION is one number."]},"6.3":{"s":"The rate law gives rate = k[A]^x[B]^y, where x and y are found by experiment, not from the balanced equation. The order of reaction is x + y. k is the rate constant, and its units depend on the order. Rate depends on concentration; k does not.","f":[{"x":"rate = k*[A]^x*[B]^y","n":"Rate law; x, y found by experiment; overall order = x + y.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.3, p.123"}],"w":["Order can be zero or a fraction (CH₃CHO decomposition is 3/2 order).","Units of k change with order: mol L⁻¹ s⁻¹ (zero), s⁻¹ (first), L mol⁻¹ s⁻¹ (second)."]},"6.4":{"s":"An elementary reaction happens in one step. Its molecularity is the number of particles that collide in that step: 1, 2 or rarely 3. Complex reactions go through several elementary steps. The slowest step is the rate-determining step. Order is found by experiment; molecularity comes from the mechanism.","w":["Molecularity is always a whole number (1, 2, 3) and only defined for an elementary step."]},"6.5":{"s":"Integrated rate laws give concentration against time. Zero order: [A] falls in a straight line, and t½ = [A]₀/(2k). First order: k = (2.303/t) log([A]₀/[A]), and t½ = 0.693/k, the same at any starting concentration. Radioactive decay is first order. A pseudo-first-order reaction behaves as first order because one reactant is in large excess.","f":[{"x":"k = (2.303/t)*log_10([A]_0/[A]_t)","n":"First order integrated rate law.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.126"},{"x":"[A]_t = [A]_0*e^(-k*t)","n":"First order: concentration falls exponentially.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.126"},{"x":"t_(1/2) = 0.693/k","n":"First order half life: independent of the starting concentration.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.127"},{"x":"k = (2.303/t)*log_10(P_i/(2*P_i - P))","n":"First order gas reaction A(g) -> B(g) + C(g); P_i = initial pressure, P = total pressure at time t.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.128"},{"x":"k*t = [A]_0 - [A]_t","n":"Zero order integrated rate law; units of k are mol L^-1 s^-1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.129"},{"x":"t_(1/2) = [A]_0/(2*k)","n":"Zero order half life: proportional to the starting concentration.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.5, p.130"}],"w":["First order: a plot of log[A] against t is a straight line of slope −k/2.303.","Zero order: [A] against t is a straight line of slope −k."]},"6.6":{"s":"Molecules must collide to react, but most collisions do nothing. A collision works only if the molecules have at least the activation energy (enough to climb the energy barrier) and the right orientation. So the rate is far smaller than the collision rate."},"6.7":{"s":"Most reactions go faster when heated, often about twice as fast for a 10 °C rise, because many more molecules then have the activation energy. Arrhenius equation: k = A e^(−Ea/RT). Plotting log k against 1/T gives a straight line, from which Ea can be found.","f":[{"x":"k = A*e^(-E_a/(R*T))","n":"Arrhenius equation; A = pre-exponential factor, E_a = activation energy, T in kelvin.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.7, p.132"},{"x":"log_10(k) = -E_a/(2.303*R)*(1/T) + log_10(A)","n":"Arrhenius plot: log k against 1/T is a line of slope -E_a/(2.303 R).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.7, p.132"},{"x":"log_10(k_2/k_1) = (E_a/(2.303*R))*((T_2 - T_1)/(T_1*T_2))","n":"Activation energy from rate constants at two temperatures.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 6.7, p.133"}],"w":["Use R = 8.314 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ with the activation energy in joules, not kilojoules."]},"6.8":{"s":"A catalyst speeds up a reaction without being used up. It gives the reaction a different path with a lower activation energy. It does not change ΔH or the position of equilibrium; it only helps equilibrium be reached sooner. Enzymes are biological catalysts.","w":["A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward and backward reactions equally, so K does not change."]}};
